-- title:
--   Extending x ↦ c to L(x,y) → A along a root
-- statement:
--   Let $L$, $F$, $A$ be fields with $F$ and $A$ both $L$-algebras, let $x, y \in F$, and suppose $x$ is transcendental over $L$. Let $P \in \mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ be monic (as a polynomial in $Y$ over $\mathbb{Z}[X]$) and assume that the minimal polynomial of $y$ over the intermediate field $L(x) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin L {x}` is the image of $P$ under the coefficientwise map $\mathbb{Z}[X] \to L(x)$ determined by the canonical ring homomorphism $\mathbb{Z} \to L(x)$ and by $X \mapsto x$; that is, $\operatorname{minpoly}_{L(x)}(y) = P(x, Y)$. Let further $c, z \in A$ with $c$ transcendental over $L$, and assume $z$ is a root of the polynomial $P(c, Y) \in A[Y]$ obtained from $P$ by the analogous specialisation $\mathbb{Z}[X] \to A$, $X \mapsto c$. The conclusion is that there exists an $L$-algebra homomorphism $\psi$ from the intermediate field $L(x,y) =$ `IntermediateField.adjoin L {x, y}` of $F$ into $A$ which sends the element $x$ to $c$ and the element $y$ to $z$ (the two arguments of $\psi$ being $x$ and $y$ together with their membership in $L(x,y)$).
--
--   This is the standard extension criterion for embeddings of a field generated by a transcendental element and an element algebraic over it: an $L$-isomorphism $L(x) \cong L(c)$ coming from transcendence transports the minimal polynomial $P(x,Y)$ to $P(c,Y)$, and a chosen root $z$ of the latter determines an extension to $L(x,y)$. It is used in the construction of the reciprocity maps attached to modular polynomial data, via [`ModularCurve.LambdaModularPolynomialData.psi_reciprocal`](thm.html#ModularCurve.LambdaModularPolynomialData.psi_reciprocal).
-- source:
